Ellen master thank you so much for  joining me today thanks for having me I  am gonna show the book here because this  I find this is just a great book I  enjoyed this thoroughly thank you so  much for saying that what a lot of  people may not realize is if they've  watched that old black and white movie  about time travel where the guy has to  drag the time machine because he goes  back in time but time has changed he  ends up where he doesn't want to be  inside a building somewhere in the past  that in fact is is not the way it works  and I really never thought about it  until I read your book but if we went  back just a few seconds to the beginning  of when I started this interview we  wouldn't be here we wouldn't be in some  place ten seconds ago where would we be  well yeah that's one of the things I had  fun exploring the book is that we feel  like we're stationary right now we're  sitting here doing this interview but  we're actually moving in incredible  speeds we know that the earth is  spinning at the equator at about a  thousand miles an hour it's rotating  around the Sun at about 67,000 miles an  hour the Sun is moving through the  galaxy at 1.3 million miles an hour so  right now if we were to go back to  seconds we would be about half a  kilometer away and so I like the idea of  exploring among many things in the book  that when you travel back in time you  also have to travel back in space  because if you were to go back to  yesterday the earth would be thousands  and thousands of kilometres away we  might end up somewhere in the middle of  space so you would I mean you know right  where we're sitting right now wouldn't  be in the same place we would be out in  the empty vacuum of space and so unless  you have a model of time travel that  also considers not just that the earth  is spinning incredibly quickly but it's  moving at unbelievable speeds you go  back you know 30 years like Marty McFly  did in Back to the Future you don't end  up landing in the exact same spot where  you took off you end up floating in the  vacuum of space with the earth a million  minus millions and millions of miles  away yeah and so that is one of many  sort of fun sort of ways of looking at  the genre in with fresh eyes you know  try to come up with a model of time  travel it isn't just walking through a  door but one that actually factors in  orbital mechanics  right where'd you get the idea for a  book on time travel well  I when I was growing out my grandfather  who was a chemist I grew up in Vancouver  he had this extensive collection of old  vintage science fiction from the 50s and  60s  Bradbury Heinlein Asimov Banach yet all  the kind of classics and I just loved  them and I'm not just the sort of wild  and vivid stories inside them but also I  just love the covers you know what these  garishly painted images of you know sign  that scientists and robots and  futuristic cityscapes rocket ships and  exactly you know and I'm but even as a  kid in the 80s I knew that the future  wasn't turning out exactly the way these  writers and artists imagined you know  yeah I I did not get a jetpack for my  12th birthday you know that seemed like  a terrible crime but don't have Jetsons  flying cars either we don't and the  other thing that happened to me when I  in 86 in Vancouver Expo 86 was hosted I  know it was a World's Fair like you know  all the great World's Fairs like  Montreal 67 and Expo and the World's  Fair in New York in 64 and I was dazzled  by and it captured my imagination I  spent a ton of time on the expo grounds  it's only recently that I would look  back on it and did a little bit of  research and realized Expo 86 was  actually the last World's Fair we ever  hosted in North America in fact we  stopped posting them here it's like we  stopped dreaming of that future but I  always was always thought about that  question what happened to the future we  imagined was going to happen what went  wrong why are we here in 2016 it's very  different than the one that are that  your post-war generation imagined and so  that that that thought was the genesis  of this book and then I kind of went  from there the whole idea of course is  if you go and and this remains a big  question for anybody who thinks about  this is if you go and back into the past  even it's kind of like the butterfly  effect that's right yeah  even a minor detail can vastly change  changes in the future right and in my  book when the character goes back it he  doesn't cause a minor change he causes a  massive change and and he wakes he ends  up in a very different version of the  world but one of the twists of the book  is that a very different version of the  world that he wakes up in is our world  the book is no big when the book opens  it's in the present but it's in this  of the present that the post-war  generation imagined the sort of techno  utopian fantasy flying cars and robot  maids and hotels on the moon  teleportation but with there's a you  know a terrible mistake with a prototype  time machine and my protagonist causes  this accident in 1965 and when he  returns to the present he wakes up an  hour version of the world what we  perceived to be the real world but for  him is this terrible dystopia where  everything has gone wrong what happened  to our flying cars what happened to our  jetpacks so of course he wants to get  back to his version of the world but  along the way he discovers these very  unexpected versions of the people in his  life his family this woman he has this  romantic connection with and even  himself he's a very different person and  I like the idea of looking at the world  around us with fresh eyes bringing  somebody from a much more sophisticated  I mean technologically sophisticated not  necessarily sophisticated in all ways  but a technologically advanced version  of the world and dropping him into our  world and having a look around and said  what happened you make it very  interesting as well by using some actual  technological facts yeah how much  research did you have to do to get these  things are quite a bit um you know the I  mentioned my grandfather as a scientist  you know one of his kind of gripes about  the science fiction genre is that you  know you have all these great stories  they use all these ideas but they never  actually took the time to get the  science right and if they would just get  the science right he would say to me  this the stories would actually be more  interesting because the science is  fascinating and so when I set out to  write this book I you know because the  inspiration came from my grandfather I  wanted to honor that imperative as well  so yeah I mean I don't put all of my  research in the book like I didn't want  a situation where the reader has to wade  through pages and pages of scientific  research obviously this is a novel it's  a story first but I liked working a lot  of real scientific details technological  details in there because I just feel  like it grounds of the story makes it  more textured or more authentic  mmm in the end the cover it you if maybe  people don't notice this right away but  you have the skyline of Toronto yeah and  then upside down you have the sky later  Toronto so is it is a case of a lot of  people wonder whether there are  alternate or alternative realities  but you're saying this is sort of like  it could go this way or that way yeah  yeah I mean I'm very interested like a  lot of people in the what ifs of life  what you know what a decision you could  have made at some point in your life  that was kind of like a hinge that  changed the trajectory of your life but  I also think because of my personal  family history I think a lot about how  who we are ends up being the result of  historical forces that are far beyond  our own small lives you know and many of  my grandfather grew up in the great  depression and that profoundly  influenced the trajectory of his life  my grandmother had grew up in Vienna and  had to escape the Nazis in the war and  ended up in North America my father as  well as a child he was born in Morocco  and ended up having to escape from from  World War two into France and then his  by the time he was 10 years old he had  lived on three different continents he  was in Israel and I think a lot about  you know when I was growing up around  the dinner table everybody had a  different accent I was born in Vancouver  but I was a first generation Canadian  and just thinking about how the effects  of these giant sweeps of history Wars  and cultural revolutions and depressions  and how all these things which you read  in history books actually profoundly  influenced how I was even born of all  the people in my life conference of  history yeah and so I want to think  about that too it's not just our own  personal choices although those of  course can have profound impact but also  how a different hinge in history could  lead you to have a very different life I  must congratulate you as well Ellen must  I on the fact that you set this in  Toronto as a Canadian writer you set  this in a sort of a Canadian although  this guy travels around quite a bit yeah  I mean is there any pressure I mean to  make this more marketable by not setting  it in Canada and setting it somewhere  else  no I never I never had got that kind of  kind of pressure I mean the majority of  the story probably about 60% if it takes  place in Toronto some of it takes place  in San Francisco in Hong Kong because  his journey kind of takes him around  around the world but no it's very  proudly and specifically said in in  Toronto which is where I live right now  as a Canadian writer I just wanted to  first of all write about the place I  live my perspective is Canadian I'm  Canadian I've lived here my whole life  but also I feel like you know we're at  this point I think people are proud to  be Canadian our literature is very  mature  we have terrific writers that whose work  travels all around the world and I don't  think there's any reason to kind of like  sheepishly pretend that it's we're not  who we are I'm proud of what Canada  represents right now in the world and  even though we're a tad if slightly  different geopolitical situation than we  were when I finished the book this  summer I feel like I just wanted to  write a book um that was you know it's  there's time travel and alternate  realities and all kinds of big plot  twists and turns and characters revealed  but it's still a story that is written  by Canadian in the set here because  that's who I am  now we've been talking a lot about time  travel but this isn't necessarily a book  about time travel is it time travel is a  plot device that I used to it's really  more about alternate realities and the  consequences of our choices sometimes  understand abilities yeah and the  responsibilities we have you know I mean  a big concept I talk about in the book  is the study of the accident that the  sense that anytime you introduce a  technology you also reduce the accident  of that technology there's no such thing  as a car crash until you invent the car  there's no such thing as a plane crash  until you invent the plane and so and I  like to explore the unintended  consequences of things so both  technology but also people how you meet  somebody new  and they can have all these unintended  consequences in your life and vice versa  and so yeah to me this is really a story  about people and how once you pull all  the technology away although obviously  that stuff is fun to write about what  what you're left with is the connections  between them and how the people that we  meet the people we choose to love the  family we grew up in that's what really  gives our life meaning and that's what  fundamentally changes the trajectory of  our lives rather than messed I I must  say it was a truly fascinating book and  so I thank you very much for this  congratulations thank you and thank you  for having me on your show

